namespace Botan {

enum class Algorithm_Policy_Kind { Standard, FIPS, Custom }; // maybe

class BOTAN_PUBLIC_API(3, 10) Algorithm_Policy final {
   public:
      static Algorithm_Policy standard();       // everything compiled in
      static Algorithm_Policy fips();           // FIPS 140-3 approved subset
      static Algorithm_Policy strict();         // conservative modern subset
      static Algorithm_Policy custom(std::set<std::string> allowed); // whitelist
      bool allows(std::string_view algo_spec) const;
      std::string_view name() const;
      std::string reason(std::string_view algo_spec) const; // why rejected
};

// Global active policy (default standard)
BOTAN_PUBLIC_API void set_algorithm_policy(Algorithm_Policy p);
BOTAN_PUBLIC_API const Algorithm_Policy& algorithm_policy();

// Pre-check API
BOTAN_PUBLIC_API bool algorithm_available(std::string_view algo_spec); // compiled-in AND policy-allowed
BOTAN_PUBLIC_API void throw_if_algorithm_not_permitted(std::string_view type, std::string_view algo_spec);
}
```

Wait — "3 种策略" (3 policies). Standard / FIPS / Strict is a clean trio. Plus custom whitelist for "按策略的算法白名单"? The whitelist requirement: "按策略的算法白名单" = per-policy algorithm whitelist. So each policy has a whitelist. Custom could be a 4th. Keep it: three built-in policies (Standard = allow all, FIPS, Strict) each with a whitelist, plus optional custom whitelist. Actually to keep acceptance crisp: 3 policies = Standard, FIPS, Strict. I'll also support custom whitelist since "白名单" is explicitly requested... The whitelist is the mechanism per policy. I'll add `custom()` too — it's cheap and directly demonstrates whitelist. Hmm, but scope. The requirement says "按策略的算法白名单" — whitelist per policy. Three policies suffice. I'll include custom as a bonus? Keep minimal: three policies, each defined by whitelist (Standard's whitelist = everything → represented as "allow all"). I think adding custom() is genuinely useful and small. I'll add it.

